<ResourcePage
  title="Social Security Disability Lawyer Fees"
  description="Learn in plain language how representative fees and case expenses may work in an SSI or SSDI claim."
  intro="Ask for a written explanation of attorney fees and case expenses before you agree to representation."
  related={[
    { href: '/how-we-help', label: 'Learn how the firm may help' },
    { href: '/frequently-asked-questions', label: 'Read common disability questions' },
    { href: '/contact', label: 'Contact Williams & Swee, Ltd.' },
  ]}
>
  <!-- Fee basics: avoids promising a specific arrangement before firm review. -->
  <Heading level="h2">How fees often work</Heading>
  <p>
    Social Security must authorize most fees charged by a representative for work before the agency. One common method
    is a written fee agreement tied to past-due benefits.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rules include a percentage limit and a dollar cap. The cap can change. The agreement and facts of the case
    matter, so ask the firm for its current terms.
  </p>

  <Heading level="h2">Fees and expenses are different</Heading>
  <p>
    An attorney fee pays for legal work. Case expenses may include costs for records, reports, postage, or other items.
    Ask whether you may owe expenses even if benefits are not awarded.
  </p>

  <Heading level="h2">Questions to ask</Heading>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>How will the fee be calculated?</li>
    <li>Does Social Security need to approve the fee?</li>
    <li>What case expenses might I have to pay?</li>
    <li>Could the fee process change if my case goes beyond a hearing?</li>
    <li>When would any fee or expense be due?</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="important-note">
    <Heading level="h2" size="h4">Draft fee language</Heading>
    <p>
      Williams &amp; Swee, Ltd. must replace this section with its actual, attorney-approved fee and expense terms
      before publication.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <Link href="https://www.ssa.gov/representation/fee_agreements.htm" isExternal
      >Read Social Security’s fee-agreement information</Link
    >.
  </p>
</ResourcePage>
